-- ako/mxcli#1654 (upstream mendixlabs/mxcli#1645): `mxcli test --local`
-- generates the startup microflow MxTest.RegisterEndpoint, which registers the
-- test endpoint and then calls the project's own after-startup microflow. It was
-- written without "apply entity access", and MxBuild refuses a microflow that
-- does not apply entity access calling one that does (CE0114) — so in a project
-- whose after-startup microflow applies entity access no test could run, and the
-- error said it was "in the project, not in this run's tests".
--
-- Setup: test-runner-1654-setup.mdl. Then:
--
--   mxcli test mdl-examples/bug-tests/test-runner-1654-startup-applies-entity-access.test.mdl -p app.mpr --local
--
-- Measured on 11.13.0 (--local --db-type hsqldb). Before the fix: "build failed
-- … [CE0114] … at MxTest / Microflow 'RegisterEndpoint' / Call microflow
-- activity 'ASU_Startup'" and no test runs. After: both tests PASS — the second
-- one shows the project's startup microflow ran at boot, through the generated
-- flow. It consumes the marker it counts (`@cleanup none`), so it cannot pass on
-- an earlier boot's: its control, the same run with --skip-app-startup, FAILs
-- it ("expected $Count > 0, actual: 0").
--
-- CE0114 has one direction (mx check, 11.13.0): non-applying → applying is the
-- error; applying → non-applying and applying → applying are 0 errors, at
-- security Off and Production. So RegisterEndpoint now always applies entity
-- access, and calls a startup microflow of either kind.
mdl 1;
/**
 * @test Addition returns the sum
 * @expect $result = 4
 */
$result = call microflow MyModule.SUB_Add(A = 2, B = 2);
/

/**
 * @test the app's own after-startup microflow ran at boot
 * @cleanup none
 * @expect $Count > 0
 */
retrieve $Markers from MyModule.StartupMarker;
$Count = count $Markers;
delete $Markers;
/
